This is the doc file for "philmail"  V1.0  June 26, 1992philmail is basically a mail reader. With one exception. see "SPECIAL", below.Please read THIS ENTIRE FILE. Inportant information is contained herein.philmail does everything but delete mail articles, because that gets messy.It DOES save individual articles, however, and also reply, show titles, anda few more nifty things.Overall, I consider it a "nice" interface.For optimal use, define the environment variablesMAIL     (as your desired mail location, eg /dd/spool/mail/philb)EDITOR   (your preferred editor to use)As a default, philmail will open up your MAIL file and read the stuff therein.Also, whenever replying to mail, or just sending mail, it will attempt to fork off EDITOR. If the fork fails, it will attempt to drop back to line mode.line mode is the traditional "keep typing until done, edit only the current line"you _MUST_ _DEFINE_ the environment variable "SENDMAIL"as:   setenv SENDMAIL /dd/cmds/rmail  (or whereever)or:  play with the source in the relavant place if you want to use this on your home machine , with uucp, instead of regular "mail"**OR**:  you could rename philmail to plain old "mail", and it shouldfigure out things from there. Note:you must NOT CALL IT AS  "/dd/cmds/mail" for this to work.If you DO call it as /dd/cmds/mail, it will not work that way, unlessyou have the SENDMAIL var set.UNfortunately, this rmail feature is as yet untested by me :-(but I have confidance in my programming. Do you?  :-)     SPECIAL:     ---------I designed this as an OFF-LINE reader for UNIX systems.For this purpose, follow the following proceedure: [ It SEEMS long.. don't worry, it isn't really]  FIRST, on your UNIX system,       a) enter mail      b) s * mailfile      c) d *      d) q  You now have a file called "mailfile", with all your unread mail.    Now download "mailfile" to your home system any way you like, not  forgetting to replace UNIX linefeeds with CRs. VERY IMPORTANT.    SECOND, on your HOME system,     a) read your mail         presuming you downloaded it as "mailfile", you can:          "philmail -f mailfile"	or just           "philmail"  	   if your MAIL var is set to "mailfile"     b) reply to any mail with 'r' or 'R'.  this will make a            "mail.xxxxx" for EACH REPLY.     c) make any NEW, UNRELATED mail by          "philmail address@goes.here"     d) batch upload ALL "mail.xxxx" files.   LASTLY, on the UNIX system,     You now have a bunch of "mail.xxxx" files on your unix system.     ( DON'T FORGET TO CR->LF CONVERT)     luckily, you can very easily use a SIMPLE shell-script to send ALL     the mail files you made.     one for "csh" can be made as following -------------------------------------------------------#!/bin/csh# this is a file, I call it "sendallmail"# you must set it executable by "chmod 0755 sendallmail"# The "-t" for sendmail, makes it look in the mail for who to send it to#   instead of telling it on the command lineforeach i (mail.*)/usr/lib/sendmail -t <$iecho sent "$i"end# end of file  ---------------------------------------------#!/bin/sh# this is the SAME THING, but using "sh". use EITHER# this file, or the above file.for i in mail.*do/usr/lib/sendmail -t <"$i"echo send "$i"done#end of file  --------------------------------------------------I know the csh file works.. the /bin/sh file is a guess.note that YOUR sendmail may be in a different place. but it shouldn't be.   ****************************************CoCo Porting notes:Okay, folks for all getenv() values, you're going to have to hardcode.No big deal. just go for it.Also, you're going to have to hardcode the char * "progname".OOPS: ALSO,  in mail(), "readmail.c"  you are going to have to substitute something for _getsys();  No big deal. I use that to make a temporary filename.  You can just use anything that gets time in seconds.Sorry: I do not have an active coco, so I ain't about to go fiddle and compile it.So give it a shot yourself! Compile, and enjoy, I hope.